  "notes": "The fastest mainstream country catalogue of the era, and the entry says outright that this is \"the band that defines the top of the archetype's tempo band for the engine\". `bpm` therefore spans the stated 54–198 with `mode` at 176 (\"Small Talk\"), and the entry defends the top readings explicitly: \"the 176–198 readings are genuine, not doubled artefacts — at that band the snare stream is straight 8ths and the harmony rate is one chord per bar\", which is why `harmonicRhythm` is weighted 6:1 to one chord per bar. The drum grammar is the entry's simplification rule: \"above ~170 the train beat simplifies: the ghost-note density drops, the stream becomes a driven 8th chug with the backbeat hard on top, kick alternates two-beat and quarters, and the hat is closed on quarters or absent\". So `velocityTiers` push the accent to 108–127 while the ghost band drops to 26–40 (the widest accent-to-ghost spread here), `kick` is [2,4] on anchors 1 and 3, and the hat is `base` \"4th\" with `continuous` false so it can be absent. The bass follows \"at 180+ the bass reduces to roots on 1 and 3 because anything busier stops reading\": `followRootsProb` 0.96 on mirror_kick with the narrowest passing-tone set in the batch. Harmony is the stated I–IV–V, I–♭VII–IV on the rockers and a twelve-bar frame on the trucker/boogie covers, authored as the I–IV–I–V–IV–I skeleton at `maxChords` 6. ⚠ INFERRED: velocity tiers, `percs` contents, the humanize figures and every melody and countermelody number. \"High-energy, rhythmically dense tenor with chanted, crowd-answerable chorus cells\" is the source for `structure` call_response and the 6–11 density, and both are this author's reading. ⚠ Tempo readings are [LOW-CONF] as a class and the entry states \"harmonic and palette detail generalised [LOW-CONF]\".",
